WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.500
UMAP.

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:06.633
Like t-SNE, but faster, and the best tool for looking inside an embedding space.

3
00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:09.900
UMAP plays the same game as t-SNE.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.337
It projects non-linearly, keeping local neighborhoods,

5
00:00:13.337 --> 00:00:16.300
but it also preserves more of the global structure.

6
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:20.333
And it can transform new points, which t-SNE classically cannot.

7
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:23.733
Here is how UMAP works, in two moves.

8
00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:29.445
First it links every point to its nearest neighbours in the original high dimensional space,

9
00:00:29.445 --> 00:00:30.445
building a graph.

10
00:00:30.445 --> 00:00:33.033
The colors mark which points are neighbours.

11
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:37.992
Second, it lays the points out in two dimensions, starting from a random scatter.

12
00:00:37.992 --> 00:00:42.133
Linked points are pulled together, and unlinked points are pushed apart.

13
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:45.589
Repeat that, and neighbourhoods settle into islands.

14
00:00:45.589 --> 00:00:50.200
It is much faster than t-SNE, and scales to hundreds of thousands of points.

15
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.067
The placement of islands is somewhat more meaningful, though still not literal distance.

16
00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.567
Here is the killer app for an AI engineer.

17
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:01.633
Take your text, headlines or document chunks.

18
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:04.400
Embed them into high dimensional vectors.

19
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.267
Project them to two dimensions with UMAP.

20
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:08.218
Then look.

21
00:01:08.218 --> 00:01:10.108
Do topics form clean islands?

22
00:01:10.108 --> 00:01:11.933
Where do duplicates pile up?

23
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:14.467
Two knobs matter most.

24
00:01:14.767 --> 00:01:20.100
N neighbors: small values show local detail, large values show the global shape.

25
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.967
And min dist controls how tightly points pack.

26
00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:25.700
In code, we embed first.

27
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.100
Two hundred space news headlines become three hundred eighty four dimensional embeddings.

28
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.267
Then UMAP projects them to two dimensions, and we scatter the result.

29
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:38.067
Here is a debugging story.

30
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:42.900
Mission Control RAG keeps retrieving irrelevant chunks for propulsion queries.

31
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.667
You UMAP the vector store, and propulsion and electrical power are fused into one island.

32
00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:51.667
The chunking split documents mid section.

33
00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.500
Re-chunk, re-embed, and retrieval improves.

34
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:56.506
Let's recap.

35
00:01:56.506 --> 00:02:00.267
UMAP is faster than t-SNE, with better global structure.

36
00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:02.133
It can transform new points.

37
00:02:02.433 --> 00:02:04.167
Use it to eyeball your embeddings.

38
00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:05.967
Next up: accuracy.
